  "summary": "Large-scale mosaic artworks integrated into the facades and public spaces of Soviet buildings, serving both decorative and ideological purposes.",
  "description": "These mosaics featured vibrant colors, socialist themes, and idealized depictions of workers, industry, and Soviet achievements. They emerged as an accessible art form that could withstand harsh climate conditions while conveying propaganda messages.",
  "culturalContext": "Soviet architectural mosaics (1960s-1980s) — Kyiv's \"golden mile\" murals, Tashkent's ceramic facades, Palanga's sanatorium panels — fused Smalti smalt glass and tile into socialist optimism: astronauts, workers, sunbursts and folk-motif patterns covering airport, hotel and institute walls. Artists (Ada Rybachuk, Vladimir Melnichenko, Uzbek workshops) made the USSR's largest public-art program; post-Soviet demolition debates (Kyiv's \"protect the mosaics\" campaigns) now treat them as endangered modern heritage.",
